  "strategy": "### Approach and Rationale  \nThe current approach uses SLSQP and gradient-based optimization, but the Erds minimum overlap problem is highly non-convex and requires escaping local optima. This strategy adapts a multi-resolution framework using physics-informed basis functions. The key idea is to represent the step function using piecewise constant components over a hierarchy of time scales (resolutions), allowing deterministic dynamics to simulate exploration. This approach combines adaptive basis functions with projected gradient steps to enforce constraints and escape local minima. The algorithm balances exploration/exploitation and leverages the inductive bias of sparse representations to achieve improvements with less local-search intensity compared to random restarts.  \n\n### Mathematical Specification  \nThe step function \\( h(x) \\) on \\([0, 2]\\) is approximated by sparse basis functions:  \n\\[ h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^N c_i \\cdot g_i(x, \\theta) \\]  \n- \\(c_i\\) are coefficients (weights satisfying \\(\\sum_i c_i = 1\\) and \\(c_i \\geq 0\\))  \n- \\(g_i(x, \\theta)\\) are piecewise basis functions (triangular pulses) parameterized by sparse sets \\(\\Theta\\)  \n- \\(N\\) is the number of non-zero coefficients, dynamically adjusted  \n\nThe objective and constraints:  \n\\[ \\min_{c \\in \\mathbb{R}_+, N} \\max_k \\left( \\int h(x) (1 - h(x+k)) \\, dx \\right) \\]  \nsubject to:  \n\\[ 0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1, \\quad \\int_0^2 h(x) \\, dx = 1 \\]  \n\nThe basis functions are defined as:  \n\\[ g_i(x, \\Theta) = \\max\\left(0, \\theta_{i,a} - |x - \\theta_{i,b}| \\varphi\\right) \\quad \\text{for} \\quad \\theta_{i,a}, \\theta_{i,b} \\in \\Theta \\]  \n\n### Implementation Procedure  \n1. Validate parent parent_h = initial_h_values (n=336)  \n2. Initialize parameters:  \n   - Resolution hierarchy: \\(n = 672, 1344, 2688\\) (upscaling for refined search)  \n   - Basis function specifications: sparse triangular pulses (\\(\\varphi = 0.2\\))  \n   - Dynamics parameters: mutation rate 0.3, cooling schedule \\(T(t) = T_0 \\log(1/t)\\)  \n   - Local search budget: 30 projected gradient steps  \n\n3. For each resolution level (descending order):  \n   a. Interpolate parent representation to target resolution  \n   b. Initialize basis sets:  \n        - Batch 1: 100 random sparse sets \\(\\Theta\\)  \n        - Batch 2: 100 interpolated copies of parent \\(\\Theta\\)  \n\n   c. Run adaptive gradient dynamics:  \n        \\[ \\nabla_c L(c) = \\left( \\frac{1}{c_{\\text{max}}} \\right) \\partial_c \\max_k \\text{correlation}(h) \\]  \n        Projected gradient step:  \n        \\[ c_{\\text{new}} = \\frac{c}{\\|c\\|} \\cdot \\min\\left( N_{\\text{max}}, \\frac{\\|c\\|^2}{N}\\right) \\]  \n\n   d. Prune degenerate solutions: keep top 5 basis sets (lowest correlation)  \n\n4. Final solution:  \n   - Up-scaling candidate function  \n   - If \\((2.0/n) \\cdot \\max_k \\text{correlation} < 0.38085\\), accept",
